  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Test what the sport needs', note: 'Validity first: a vertical jump for volleyball, VO₂max for distance runners, repeat-sprint ability for footballers. The testing battery mirrors the energy lesson\'s demands — measuring is only useful if it measures the event\'s currency.' },
    { math: '2 · Reliability decides meaning', note: 'A test with ±5% error can\'t detect a 3% improvement — typical error of measurement, not just correlation with performance, decides whether change is signal (the statistics lesson\'s measurement error, athlete edition).' },
    { math: '3 · The athlete-management stack', note: 'GPS/load monitoring (the biomechanics lesson), wellness questionnaires, HRV (autonomic readiness — imperfect but cheap), blood markers (ferritin, vitamin D) — fused into a readiness dashboard; the coach still decides, the dashboard prevents the coach\'s optimism from overruling the data.' },
    { math: '4 · Analytics changed team sport', note: 'Moneyball (2003) proved market inefficiencies in baseball; expected goals (xG) now explains football beyond the scoreboard; GPS-derived running profiles shape substitution and recruitment — the data-science lesson\'s predictive modelling, wearing boots.' },
