---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/sports-science/expert/testing-and-analytics-in-elite-sport">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Testing and analytics is <strong>the statistics and data-science ladders, run inside a
    club</strong>: valid, reliable measurement of the physiological and tactical variables that
    decide selection, load and tactics — and the honest handling of noise that separates
    insight from superstition.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="validity, reliability, the readiness stack, analytics" widget="equationStep" config={testingCfg} />

  <h2>Expert-level realities</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Selection analytics has limits:</strong> player metrics interact (the network lesson applies to passes — "pass networks" model teams as graphs); the best clubs combine analytics with scouting eyes because character and coachability still resist the numbers.</li>
    <li><strong>Injury prediction remains mostly a warning light:</strong> models find risk factors, not individual futures (rare-event prediction — the statistics lesson\'s base-rate problem); the useful product is flagging load spikes for conversation, not benching players by algorithm.</li>
    <li><strong>Anti-doping is a testing programme:</strong> biological passports (longitudinal blood markers — an anomaly-detection system), whereabouts rules, intelligence-led testing; the arms race runs both ways (detection vs evasion — the gene-doping question stays ahead of assays).</li>
    <li><strong>Paralympic classification is measurement with ethics:</strong> impairments are measured and classes assigned to equalise — contested, refined, and one of sport-science\'s most consequential measurement problems.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Lewis, Moneyball (2003); WADA athlete biological passport documentation; Hopkins on test reliability; the statistics ladder.</p>
</TopicPage>
